cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Water Scarcity in Morocco: A Multivariate Analysis of Climatic, Agricultural, and Socioeconomic Drivers"
message: "If you use this research, data, or code, please cite as below."
type: software
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-11-02"
license: MIT
abstract: |
  An exploratory associational analysis of the climatic, agricultural,
  and socioeconomic drivers of national freshwater withdrawals in Morocco
  (2000-2025). The study uses five publicly available World Bank
  indicators, supplemented by national indicators from Morocco's 2025
  State of the Environment Report, and applies ordinary least squares
  regression with full diagnostics (VIF, condition number, confidence
  intervals).

  Key findings include a model R-squared of 0.812 on a sample of n = 16,
  with agricultural water demand emerging as the dominant driver of
  withdrawal trajectories. Morocco's renewable freshwater per capita
  fell from approximately 2,560 cubic meters per year in 1960 to about
  620 cubic meters in 2020, well inside the United Nations absolute
  water-scarcity threshold.

  The analysis is explicitly exploratory and associational. It is not
  designed to establish causal attribution, and its conclusions should
  be read as hypothesis-generating rather than confirmatory.
keywords:
  - water scarcity
  - morocco
  - world bank indicators
  - OLS regression
  - environmental science
  - water policy
  - agricultural water demand
  - climate change
  - freshwater withdrawals
